M. Cao (mcao@u.washington.edu) wrote:
: 
: The filesystem on my Digital Unix machine is Advfs, and rdump does not
: support Advfs.       
: 
: Can you tell me how to backup my system to the remote tape drive ?
: 
: Thanks in advance
: 
: Minh
: 

Actually there is a way to do remote vdump's.

The answer is basically to run the vdump(8) command on the
local system with the output sent to stdout. The stdout is
then piped to a remote shell to the remote system running
a dd(1) command to it's local tape device. You will need to
specify the block size for the whole thing though. Also you
will need to grant trusted host access to the local system on 
the remote system (/.rhosts file).

Here's an example vdump command to vdump the root partition:

# vdump -0 -u -b 64 -f - / | rsh [remote_hostname] dd of=/dev/nrmt0h bs=64k


The vrestore(8) works in reverse fashion, but the pseudo-shell
that the interactive option of vrestore invokes will not work
with an rsh. A full partition vrestore without the -i will
work with rsh though.

This example will restore the entire vdump archive located on the
specified tape drive:

# rsh [remote_hostname] dd if=/dev/rmt0h bs=64k | vrestore -x -f -

Matt
