Organization: Imperial College
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.misc


Normally you must be the superuser to access the cd-rom.

As root run "file /dev/rrz*c" and look for RRD line in output -
usually /dev/rz4 or /dev/rz5 - this will not work for a normal
account.

If it isn't there, shut down to the boot prompt and enter "sh dev",
listing the scsi devices. Find the cd-rom and note scsi address,
dka400, dkb500 depending on which scsi the cd-rom is attached to.
Boot system and in /dev check that the corresponding device exist,
i.e., dka400 would be rrz4 and dkb500 would be rrz13 (eight is added
for the external scsi).
Use the /dev/MAKEDEV command to create the device if its not in /dev.
	cd /dev
	./MAKEDEV rz4
This should create rrz4a rrz4b rrz4c .... etc.
Then run "file /dev/rrz*c" again.

Depending on the CD format try and assuming device rz4 try ...
	mount -r /dev/rz4c /mnt
	mount -r /dev/rz4a /mnt
	mount -vt cdfs -o ro,noversion /dev/rz4c /mnt
	mount -vt cdfs -o ro,noversion /dev/rz4a /mnt

Hopefully one of the above will work.
-- 
.

                         Jeff Wilson
                         London - UK

